    difficultyVerbatim: `Reader: It seems that the number 3 governs a person's interest in technical and exact sciences. Is this true, and why are these particular sciences given precedence?
Author: Before we address the relationship between the number 3 and the sciences, let us first clarify what "science" actually is. Science originated at a time when humans faced difficulties regarding food, warmth, means of protection and transportation, and the fulfillment of other basic needs. Much to the chagrin of those in the humanities, it was precisely out of these technical human necessities that science was born; consequently, it evolved into the realm of the exact sciences (mathematics, physics, chemistry).
As for the humanities, they tend to engage more with the spheres of the soul and memory. They are primarily concerned with questions such as: "Why did things happen this way?", "When and how did these events unfold?", "How was the event perceived by outside observers?", and so on. It is precisely on this basis that we can assert that the number 3 signifies an interest in the exact and technical sciences.
However, if a person with a high number of 3s in their psychomatrix chooses to pursue the humanities, they will inevitably find themselves gravitating back toward the themes of exact sciences and technology—albeit from the perspective of their historical development or application. Such scholars actively employ logic, inductive reasoning, and other mathematical methods in their work.
Reader: It remains completely unclear how the number 3 could possibly influence the "physical diagonal" and other lines within the matrix.
Author: The answer to that question is not nearly as complex as you might imagine. Let us assume that an individual possesses a significant number of 3s in their psychomatrix. Since the number 3 is a component of the physical diagonal, this implies that the individual seeks variety—specifically in terms of techniques and approaches—within their intimate life. The same cannot be said for those who possess no 3s at all (for whom the emotional aspect of intimacy holds far greater importance). I am convinced that all individuals who possess a high number of threes are inventive cooks and tailors—people who pay close attention to design and technical experimentation, a trait that also makes them connoisseurs of both food and fashion. They rarely rely on pre-established recipes; instead, they most often create their own "signature" dishes (sometimes with mixed results). I am firmly convinced that all the attributes I have listed instill self-confidence in a person and, consequently, boost their self-esteem—which, in turn, reinforces their overall stability.
Reader: I would like to know: under what circumstances does the presence of the number 3 signify an inclination toward technology and the exact sciences?
Author: If a person has two or more threes, they will indeed feel an interest in these fields of study. However, pursuing them professionally is an entirely different matter. To do that, a person must possess two or more fives (representing logic) within their psychomatrix. Only by possessing strong logic can one truly master the intricacies of technology and the exact sciences. Furthermore, if the number 6 is also present, the individual will possess the ability to build and create things with their own hands.
Reader: Would it be accurate to say that the number 3 is a component of human intelligence—that it plays a role in assessing a person's mental acuity?
Author: The second part of your question is technically incorrect, as the number 9 is the one responsible for intellect and clairvoyance. As for whether the number 3 contributes to the concept of intelligence, that is indeed true. It acts as a connecting link between the number 5 (logic) and the number 9 (intellect, memory, and clairvoyance), thereby establishing the essential intuitive foundation required for creativity. One can state with confidence that a person's overall level of intelligence depends on the combined quantity of threes, fives, and nines they possess; moreover, the specific qualitative aspects of that intelligence are determined by the interplay and ratio of these numbers relative to one another. The greater the total number of these digits, the higher the individual's intelligence.
Reader: Is it possible—by generating an additional "three" through technical pursuits—to strengthen the "carnal diagonal"? After all, the addition of a single digit alters its qualitative nature quite noticeably.
Author: I don’t think that by taking up car repair, you will suddenly become "hotter" in bed; the "three" you’ve generated in this instance does not belong to the carnal diagonal. It was created out of an interest in mechanics and, therefore, cannot manifest within that specific diagonal. The advice in this case is simple: you shouldn't be studying automobiles, but rather the *technique* of conjugal life. If you do that, the "three" generated in this manner will integrate into the diagonal and strengthen it.
Reader: Is it possible to "cultivate" additional "threes"?
Author: Many things are possible, but the question should be phrased differently: *is it necessary* to "cultivate" additional "threes"? Consider this: when a housewife performs a multitude of tasks in the kitchen while preparing a meal, does she lament the fact that she has too few hands and eyes—reasoning that, were she better equipped, she could get everything done faster and produce even more?
Let’s assume her wish is granted, and she suddenly possesses ten hands—along with ten eyes, just to ensure she doesn't accidentally slice the fingers off one of those extra hands. She prepares a hundred different dishes and waits for her beloved—for it was *for him* that she strove so hard, going so far as to conjure up these extra hands and eyes. She is delighted; the table is groaning under the weight of the feast. Then comes a knock at the door; her beloved enters and beholds his "beautiful darling"—who happens to have... Well, he spent the remainder of his life in a psychiatric hospital.
Yes, that is a joke—but how else can one explain to every individual that they already possess a unique set of innate qualities, and that one should not squander one’s entire life only to end up with a pathetic monstrosity—the very thing one spent a lifetime creating? Just imagine it: a person born to be a genius poet, yet who spends his entire life building shoddy automobiles—simply because he lacks the skill to build good ones.`
