    difficultyVerbatim: `Reader: Is it correct to assume that all the complexities involved in interpreting the number 4 have already been covered in our discussion regarding the number 2?
Author: Yes, you are right. Given that for any individual—whenever the need arises—the numbers 2 and 4 can transform into one another, all the advice provided regarding the number 2 applies equally to the number 4.
Reader: Why do people who have multiple 4s in their psychomatrix fall ill—sometimes with severe conditions? They ought to be in perfect health at all times.
Author: There are many reasons why severe illnesses may arise, but I will attempt to list the primary ones.
1. If a woman has a 4, or a 44 (or more), in her psychomatrix, she must bear this in mind: she is strictly advised against having abortions. Doing so can trigger the development of tumors (such as fibroids, cysts, or mastopathy).
If, however, this cannot be avoided, the abortion should ideally be performed on days when the risk of natural miscarriage is highest (maternity clinics typically have specialists well-versed in this subject); yet, even this offers no absolute guarantee. The best course of action is either to practice active contraception or to choose to have the child.
2. As the Chinese proverb goes: "It is permissible to feel tired, but one must never overexert oneself to the point of physical depletion." The number 4 falls within the column associated with providing for one's family.
If an individual neglects their health—becoming obsessively focused on this duty of provision—then their 4s are effectively "consumed." Work will completely drain a person's health if they pour every ounce of their energy into it.
3. When a person possesses a high number of 4s, it inevitably results in a heightened "Line of Purposefulness." This is a positive trait—provided that the individual has chosen a path that aligns with their natural aptitudes and talents.
Howsoever, if their ambitions are unrealistic or excessive—and they pursue their objectives by constantly overtaxing themselves or by battling against the resistance of others—the negative consequences will not be long in coming. The loss of health is inevitable, as the number 4 will be completely "absorbed" by the Line of Purposefulness. Consequently, when choosing a life goal, one must carefully assess one's own strength.
4. A person has avoided the errors associated with points 1–3—the three preceding points—yet still falls ill. It is essential to remember that there exists a punishment for human cruelty, coarseness, the neglect of one's duty toward parents, excessive authoritarianism, and intolerance toward others. This punishment is reflected in the transition of the number 8 into 11, accompanied by the loss of 22 (or 4). The unjustified exertion of power leads to a loss of health. Such illnesses cannot be cured through medication (nor, indeed, through other conventional means).
There is only one way out of this situation: to eliminate the cause of the punishment—to forgive, to remember one's parents, to harbor no ill will toward others, to refrain from suppressing others, and so on. All those engaged in healing—whether doctors or folk healers—must bear in mind that treating such "patients" is strictly forbidden: this is their cross to bear and their punishment, and it can be lifted only by the entity that imposed it—a level of authority beyond human control (God, the Higher Intellect, or Nature).
5. A person has violated none of the four points listed above, yet still falls ill; moreover, they are kind, responsive, attentive, and tolerant toward their loved ones. In this instance, the illness is caused by *porcha* (a hex or curse) or the *sglaz* (the evil eye)—specifically, through incantations or sorcery. Treatment is absolutely necessary; however, modern medicine is powerless in such cases. One must seek out "wise women" or traditional healers who employ their own specific methods (for every culture possesses its own unique ways of combating such afflictions).`
