    difficultyVerbatim: `Reader: Is it fair to say that a person with a "66" in their psychomatrix performs any task far better—and to a higher standard—than someone who lacks the number 6 entirely?
Author: A serious misconception lies at the heart of that question. If you read the book carefully, you will recall that the *absence* of the number 6 actually predisposes a person toward the arts, thereby heightening their sense of beauty. Such an individual strives to achieve beauty in everything they do, regardless of the specific task at hand. Their only potential drawback might be the *speed* at which they work, as completing a project often requires a specific creative mood.
Regarding those who possess a "66" in their psychomatrix, people often say: "Their work flows effortlessly," or "Everything sings in their hands."
Such individuals perform their work both quickly and to a high standard. Consequently, while one can certainly speak of a difference in the *speed* and *timeliness* of execution, one cannot claim there is a difference in *quality*.
Reader: Would it be accurate to say that, for anyone, it is generally better to "shed" their 6s by utilizing the 6–7 transition?
Author: When discussing numerical transitions, one must not interpret them too simplistically. The fact is, any such transition "activates" only after a specific expenditure of willpower and energetic effort. Mere desire is not sufficient to alter a numerical value. Let us imagine a person with a "66" in their psychomatrix who has mastered a craft and engages in manual labor. It might seem, at first glance, that they are merely preserving and reinforcing their "6s." Yet, if any of you has ever had the privilege of observing a true *Master* at work—in any field whatsoever—you would surely agree with me: it is a genuine pleasure to behold.
The experience is akin to witnessing the artistry of a writer wielding a pen or a painter wielding a brush.
The transition occurs precisely when a person works *creatively*—something only a true *Master* is capable of doing. At that point—once they have attained the stature of an artist, a creator, a *Master*—the transition takes place spontaneously, without any conscious act of will on their part. The answer to your question is simple: one must not "run away" from the numbers; rather, it is necessary to strive for perfection and to cultivate one's abilities and talents.
Reader: If a person's psychomatrix contains the "666..." sign, but lacks the numbers 7 and 8, can we infer that they have an inclination toward black magic, sorcery, and the desire to exert power over others?
Author: I would like to begin my answer to your question with a counter-question: "If a dog has strong teeth, does that automatically mean it is vicious and bites everyone?" I imagine that many lovers of these wonderful animals would take offense on behalf of their pets. If a person with such a psychomatrix is ​​exposed to specific circumstances from early childhood—circumstances in which they are conditioned to seek power and harbor malice—then they are indeed capable of developing the darker potential of the "666..." sign.
However, if their environment is tolerant, non-aggressive, and friendly, they tend to steer clear of this sign's negative influence and, as a rule, devote themselves to music, dance, and other forms of art. You can verify this by looking at the charts of many famous singers and actors. A person navigates through life guided by the principle of "cosmic laziness": what is the point of expending energy on conflicts and malice—especially with the "666..." sign present—when one can instead amplify one's own energy and health by making the transition from 6 to 7 (thereby gaining the "bonus" numbers 22 or 4)?`
